trailer, and the commit checks passed.\n\n| Row | Ruling | What carried the meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| LLR-248 | `[MEANING+BLESS]` | Detail now adds a refusal case: at the merge slot, a lane's claim record that can't be read is refused, naming the read, and nothing is merged. It is never read as a lane the loop did not claim. |\n| TC-242 | `[MEANING+BLESS]` | Expected and Method now verify that refusal. |\n\n`VERDICT: MEANING rows=2`, `SITTING: JUDGED kinds=amendment`\n\n- **Why it is meaning:** the old text said nothing about an unreadable claim. A builder could correctly treat a failed or garbled claim log as \"no loop claim\", and that builder fails the new text.\n- **Why I'd bless LLR-248:** SR-209 requires every commit of a loop lane to be judged. Reading an unreadable claim as unclaimed would make a claim-only loop lane look like a person's lane, which isn't judged, so the check would wave it through. The new clause makes that case refuse instead.\n- **Why I'd bless TC-242:** it checks exactly what LLR-248 adds, and nothing more.\n  - Its three new tests are in a file its Evidence cell already names. They cover the trailer-rung refusal (which leaves HEAD unmoved), the held-status-rung refusal, and the unparseable-record cases.\n  - With the six readable-log pins and the one-reader pin, I ran 11 tests and all passed.\n  - Its other cells and its `Full` tier are unchanged and still correct.\n- **Checked:** both amended cells keep their approved text exactly and only add to the end (I compared them with `tomllib`). No new file, function or symbol name enters the row text; `loop_claimed` was added only to the `code_symbol` trace cell. The sitting parser accepts the file, and the bless-mark reader returns both rows.\n\n**One advisory, not blocking:** TC-242's Method says \"a loop lane whose claim record cannot be read\". When the claim can't be read, the slot can't yet know it is a loop lane. The tests and the Expected text make clear it means any lane, so a later